extraction_notes: "WB19 Australia printed 67-70 (cache p068-p071, cache page = printed folio + 1), read in column order off the text layer. Heading The City Trader O.C.C., Non-Player Villain & Optional Player Character, at the top of printed 67 (a full-page entry start, the folio is not printed on that page); lore on printed 67-68; City Trader Background & Bonuses on printed 68-69; the stat block headed City Trader O.C.C., also known as the City Agent, on printed 69; Supply & Demand on printed 70. || PLAYABLE AND EVIL: imported as a playable class on the survey decision of 2026-10-01. Tagged evil because the book heads the class a Non-Player Villain (and the O.C.C. list on printed 56 prints City Trader O.C.C. & NPC Villain) and says all City Traders are ruthless and self-serving in the extreme. The alignment line itself is Any, but typically Anarchist or evil, which alone would not carry the tag. || GROUP: listed under Outback O.C.C.s on printed 56; occ_group optional. Base S.D.C.: 4D6 plus those from skills and bonuses is sdc_base 4d6, so no men_of_arms line is needed. || XP: the Aero-Jock, City Trader & Naval Sailor ladder on the Experience Tables, printed 224, read off a render (level 1 prints 0,000-2000). || BACKGROUND TABLE: the percentile table on printed 68-69 is stored as three variants whose names carry the band, not as a pick-one special_abilities group, because each background grants skills and a chosen ability cannot carry skills. Stored per background: the attribute bonuses (flat points and 1D4 dice), the S.D.C. dice, initiative, the Horror Factor save and the skills; the City Trader Background ability restates the table for the player. The Outsider prints Find Contraband (+15%) and Streetwise (+6%), which the O.C.C. list already grants at +16% and +4%; they are read as stacking and stored as overrides at 26+16+15=57 and 20+4+6=30. That is a reading; the other reading is that the background figure replaces the O.C.C. one (41 and 26). || SPECIAL ABILITIES: Special Trader Abilities, same as the Merchant Trader, bonuses as noted under Background (printed 69). So Evaluate the Value of Goods is the catalog Appraise Goods at 50/4 (a reading, as on the Merchant Trader), Gemology is the catalog row at 25/5, Contacts is a special ability, and the Merchant special bonuses (+1D6 M.A., +1D4 P.B., +1 initiative, +1 roll) are NOT given. || SKILLS: the O.C.C. Skills paragraph opens with the same sentence as the O.C.C. Related Skills paragraph (three Military or Espionage and four others, one more at levels 2, 4, 6, 8, 10 and 14). It is read as one grant printed twice and stored once, as seven related picks with a floor of three across Military and Espionage. Speaks Australian/English at 95% is Language: Australian English with no per-level step. Pilot: Helicopter is Helicopter; Read Sensory Equipment is Sensory Equipment; Find Contraband skill (+16%) is as printed; Literacy: English is Literacy: Native Language. Pilot: any except Robot Elite is the twelve Robot Combat Elite rows; General and Exotic Animals horsemanship are offered at the Pilot +10% because Rifts files horsemanship under Pilot, which is a reading. Cowboy: None is honoured by leaving the category out. Wilderness: Hunting is a catalog row with no percentage. || MONEY: 2D6x$1,000 in dollars and 1D4x1,000 in Universal Credits (the text layer prints 2D6x$l,000 and !D4xl,000). starting_money holds the 2D6x1000 dollars only; the further 1D4x1000 credits are in GM Notes because the field holds one expression. 3D6x$3,000 in saleable or tradeable stock is goods and is in GM Notes with the commission rule (a minimum of 2D6x10,000 per transaction; the text layer prints 2D6x 10,000). || EQUIPMENT not stored as rows: the heavy suit of environmental M.D.C. armor and the optional homespun suit for disguise (no single row is the book item), the M.D. sidearm and M.D. rifle (player choice; their sixteen E-Clips are stored), a wristwatch, a mini-pocket fan, a suitcase, satchel or briefcase with 10 M.D.C., the wardrobe, the trinkets and the vehicle are in GM Notes. || NOT STORED: the NPC-only lines (own M.D.C. helicopter with 1D4 combat features, 2-8 or 10-20 assistants, 5-10 times the money) and the Supply & Demand list are in GM Notes."
---

## Lore

A City Trader is the secret go-between of Perth or Melbourne in the Outback:
negotiator, trade organizer, customs agent, armed escort and spy in one. The
Tech-Cities need raw materials and will not admit to their own citizens that
they deal with the people outside the walls, so the trade runs through a few
hundred of these agents, who rarely say where a cargo is going or what it is.

Most were Outbackers raised to the post; some are City-born. Once outside the
walls they answer to no law, and their employers care only that the goods
arrive. They deal with townsfolk and merchants, Roadgangers, Bushrangers and
worse, and often carry out reconnaissance, supply City spies or do a little
sabotage along the way. They are wealthy, superbly equipped and well
connected. Charming or cold, the book describes them all as ruthless and
self-serving, and most Outbackers treat a community that trades with them as
traitors.

## GM Notes

The book presents this class as a non-player villain and an optional player
character; there are only a few hundred City Traders in Australia.

Background (printed 68-69): roll percentile or choose. 01-60 an Outbacker who
cut a deal with the Administration; 61-90 a Citizen of the City sent, or
punished, into the Gap, who keeps a 20% commission on what he saves the City;
91-00 an outsider loyal to neither side. The background applies its skills,
attribute bonuses, S.D.C., initiative and Horror Factor save.

Special Trader abilities the sheet does not roll for you: Contacts is 15% +4%
per level, +4% per every five M.A. points above 18. Spotting a fake with
Gemology is at -10%.

Starting kit beyond what the sheet lists: one heavy suit of environmental
M.D.C. armor (and perhaps a suit of homespun armor as a disguise), an M.D.
sidearm and an M.D. rifle, a wristwatch, a mini-pocket fan, a suitcase,
satchel or briefcase with 10 M.D.C. and personal items; nearly a dozen suits
of the finest clothes, including a trench coat, none of it fit for
cross-country travel; and trinkets to impress the locals (gum, candy,
cigarettes, cheap sunglasses, pens, markers, alcohol, a CD player and music
disks). A player character does not get a helicopter but has an M.D.C. jeep,
hovercycle or other hover vehicle, and may be able to call on more transport
and other City resources.

Money: besides the 2D6x$1,000 in dollars, 1D4x1,000 Universal Credits and
3D6x$3,000 in saleable or tradeable stock. The Trader earns a commission on
what he saves his City, a minimum of 2D6x10,000 in dollars or trade goods per
transaction, and must close at least one transaction a month or lose the
contract.

NPC City Traders have their own M.D.C. helicopter or heavy all-terrain
vehicle with 1D4 combat features, 2-8 (sometimes 10-20) assistants who are
really City troops or mercenaries, sometimes covert operatives using the
Trader as cover, and 5-10 times the money.

Supply and demand (printed 70): Perth and Melbourne want grain, metal ores,
seedlings, meat and fossil fuels; Outbackers want nearly every modern machine
and convenience; Outlanders want fuel, medicine, processed food and machine
parts; Aboriginal tribes and Reef Islanders do not trade. Ore comes from
northern Queensland; fossil fuels from NSW and Western Australia; timber from
Queensland and NSW; electronics, instruments, weapons and vehicles from Perth
and Melbourne; advanced medicine from Melbourne and Victoria Haven;
cybernetics from Melbourne and Ol'' Sydney Town.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'city-trader');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'city-trader';

-- Records this run. REQUIRED: the smoke test fails a data script that has no
-- footer, or whose footer names a different file.
INSERT INTO data_script_runs (filename) VALUES ('add-city-trader-class.sql');
